# Trigonometry: Measuring with Triangles

> One known side and one known angle unlock every other side and angle
> of a right triangle. Three ratios, one table, and a handful of
> identities have guided surveyors and sailors for two thousand years.

## 1. The Three Basic Ratios

In a right triangle, the sides of an acute angle θ are named the
**opposite** (directly across from θ), the **adjacent** (next to θ but
not the hypotenuse), and the **hypotenuse** (the longest side, opposite
the right angle).

| Ratio | Definition | Memory cue |
| --- | --- | --- |
| sin θ | opposite / hypotenuse | SOH |
| cos θ | adjacent / hypotenuse | CAH |
| tan θ | opposite / adjacent | TOA |

[definition]
For an acute angle θ in a right triangle, sine, cosine, and tangent are
the three ratios opposite/hypotenuse, adjacent/hypotenuse, and
opposite/adjacent. Together with the reciprocal ratios they form the
complete toolkit of right-triangle trigonometry.
[/definition]

[example]
A right triangle has legs 3 (opposite θ) and 4 (adjacent to θ), with
hypotenuse 5. Then sin θ = 3/5, cos θ = 4/5, and tan θ = 3/4 — three
numbers that completely describe the triangle's shape.
[/example]

The ratios depend only on the angle, not the triangle's size: a
3-4-5 triangle and a 30-40-50 triangle give identical ratios, because
scaling a triangle scales all three sides equally.

## 2. The Reciprocal Ratios

| Reciprocal | Name | Relation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| cosec θ | cosecant | 1 / sin θ |
| sec θ | secant | 1 / cos θ |
| cot θ | cotangent | 1 / tan θ = cos θ / sin θ |

For the 3-4-5 triangle above: cosec θ = 5/3, sec θ = 5/4, and
cot θ = 4/3. Each reciprocal simply flips its parent ratio.

## 3. The Standard Angles Table

| θ | 0° | 30° | 45° | 60° | 90° |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| sin θ | 0 | 1/2 | 1/√2 | √3/2 | 1 |
| cos θ | 1 | √3/2 | 1/√2 | 1/2 | 0 |
| tan θ | 0 | 1/√3 | 1 | √3 | undefined |

[tip]
The sine row hides one pattern: √0/2, √1/2, √2/2, √3/2, √4/2.
Reading that same pattern backwards gives the cosine row, and
tan = sin/cos finishes the table — three rows from one trick.
[/tip]

## 4. Finding an Unknown Side

[example]
A 10 m ladder leans against a wall at 60° to the ground. How high up
does it reach?
Step 1: the ladder is the hypotenuse; the height is the side opposite
the 60° angle.
Step 2: choose sine, because it links opposite and hypotenuse:
sin 60° = height / 10.
Step 3: height = 10 × sin 60° = 10 × (√3/2) = 5√3 ≈ 8.66 m.
[/example]

[example]
The shadow of a pole is 20 m long when the sun's elevation is 30°.
The height is opposite and the shadow adjacent, so use tangent:
height = 20 × tan 30° = 20 × (1/√3) = 20/√3 ≈ 11.55 m.
[/example]

[warning]
Sketch and label the triangle before touching numbers. Mark the known
side, the wanted side, and the angle — then pick the one ratio that
connects all three. Most trigonometry errors come from grabbing the
wrong ratio, not from arithmetic.
[/warning]

## 5. Finding an Unknown Angle

When the sides are known, read the table backwards using inverse
functions. If tan θ = 3/4, then θ = arctan(0.75) ≈ 36.9°; calculators
write the inverse keys as sin⁻¹, cos⁻¹, and tan⁻¹.

[example]
A wheelchair ramp rises 1 m over a horizontal run of 12 m.
Step 1: tan θ = 1/12 ≈ 0.083.
Step 2: θ = arctan(0.083) ≈ 4.8° — a gentle grade, comfortably inside
typical accessibility guidelines.
[/example]

[note]
Keep the calculator in degree mode for survey and navigation problems.
A radian-mode slip turns every answer into a plausible-looking wrong
number, so check the mode before each exam question.
[/note]

## 6. The Pythagorean Identity

@math label="eq:pythagorean-identity"
\sin^2\theta + \cos^2\theta = 1
@/math

It follows straight from the definitions:
(opp/hyp)² + (adj/hyp)² = (opp² + adj²)/hyp² = hyp²/hyp² = 1, where the
middle step is Pythagoras' theorem on the right triangle itself.

[example]
Given sin θ = 5/13 with θ acute, find the other ratios.
Step 1: cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ = 1 − 25/169 = 144/169.
Step 2: cos θ = 12/13 (positive, since θ is acute).
Step 3: tan θ = sin/cos = (5/13)/(12/13) = 5/12 — the 5-12-13
triangle in disguise.
[/example]

Dividing the identity through by cos²θ produces the second Pythagorean
identity 1 + tan²θ = sec²θ; dividing by sin²θ produces
1 + cot²θ = cosec²θ.

@diagram tree id="identity-family" layout="tree" aria="Family tree of the basic trigonometric identities"
  "Basic identities" -> "Pythagorean: sin^2 + cos^2 = 1"
  "Pythagorean: sin^2 + cos^2 = 1" -> "1 + tan^2 = sec^2"
  "Pythagorean: sin^2 + cos^2 = 1" -> "1 + cot^2 = cosec^2"
  "Basic identities" -> "Ratio identity: tan = sin / cos"
  "Basic identities" -> "Complementary: sin(90 - A) = cos A"
@/diagram

## 7. Complementary Angles

The two acute angles of a right triangle add to 90°, and every side is
opposite one of them and adjacent to the other. Swapping the labels
swaps the ratios:

sin(90° − θ) = cos θ  and  cos(90° − θ) = sin θ  and
tan(90° − θ) = cot θ.

[example]
Simplify sin θ · cos(90° − θ) + cos θ · sin(90° − θ).
Step 1: replace cos(90° − θ) by sin θ and sin(90° − θ) by cos θ.
Step 2: the expression becomes sin²θ + cos²θ.
Step 3: by the Pythagorean identity the value is exactly 1.
[/example]

So sin 20° = cos 70°, cos 35° = sin 55°, and any pair of "matching"
terms across 90° collapses — a shortcut examiners love to test.

## 8. Heights and Distances

The **angle of elevation** is measured up from the horizontal line of
the observer; the **angle of depression** is measured down from it.
Both are measured from the horizontal, never from the vertical, and
both set up the right triangle that trigonometry then solves.

[example]
From two points 40 m apart on a straight road, the top of a tower has
elevations 60° (near point B) and 30° (far point A). Let the tower
height be h and the near distance be x.
Step 1: near triangle — tan 60° = h/x, so x = h/√3.
Step 2: far triangle — tan 30° = h/(x + 40), so x + 40 = h√3.
Step 3: substitute: h/√3 + 40 = h√3, so 40 = h(√3 − 1/√3) = 2h/√3.
Step 4: h = 40√3/2 = 20√3 ≈ 34.6 m.
[/example]

@chart line title="sin θ for the standard angles" labels:
0°
30°
45°
60°
90°

data:
0
0.5
0.71
0.87
1
@/chart

The chart shows sine climbing smoothly from 0 to 1: near 0° a small
change of angle changes sin θ a lot, while near 90° the same change
barely moves it — one reason precision matters most at shallow
elevations.

[important]
Angles of elevation and depression are always measured from the
horizontal. A line of sight "70° from the wall" is only a 20° elevation
from the ground — reading the angle from the wrong baseline flips the
whole problem.
[/important]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Item | Result |
| --- | --- |
| sin 30°, cos 60° | both 1/2 |
| Ladder 10 m at 60° | height 5√3 ≈ 8.66 m |
| Shadow 20 m, elevation 30° | height ≈ 11.55 m |
| tan θ = 3/4 | θ ≈ 36.9° |
| sin θ = 5/13 (θ acute) | cos θ = 12/13, tan θ = 5/12 |
| sin²θ + cos²θ | 1 |
| sin 20° | equals cos 70° |
| Tower, views 60° and 30°, points 40 m apart | h = 20√3 ≈ 34.6 m |

[summary]
SOH-CAH-TOA defines the three ratios, the standard-angle table fills
them in at 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, and 90°, the Pythagorean and
complementary identities connect the ratios, and every
heights-and-distances problem is a right triangle hiding in the
landscape waiting to be labelled.
[/summary]
